## 2026-08-07 — Hero typography fixes

**What changed** (`src/components/sections/Hero.module.css` only; markup and background untouched)

- **`.heading` max-width 12ch → 56rem** (the max-w-4xl equivalent). The 12ch cap was the cause of
  the one-word-per-line stacking: the longer of the two spans, "built for rural addresses", needs
  ~709px at the 72px ceiling and 12ch allowed barely 400.
- **`.heading` line-height `var(--lead-crush)` (0.86) → 1.08.** At display size the crushed value
  was colliding ascenders with the descenders on the line above once the headline ran past two
  lines.
- **Line breaks** already come from the two block-level spans in `Hero.tsx`, so no `<br>` was
  needed — they only lacked the width to sit on one line each. `text-wrap: balance` retained for
  narrow viewports where a span genuinely does wrap. "RURAL" keeps `.accent` / `--ember-core`.
- **`.lede` max-width 38ch → 34rem**, matching `.action` exactly so the lede, the address form and
  the note beneath it read as one aligned column instead of three ragged widths.
- **`.lede` text-shadow strengthened** (three layers, 0.7/0.6/0.45 alpha). Kept as text-shadow
  rather than the literal `drop-shadow-md` equivalent — that is a UI-card shadow (0 4px 3px at 7%
  alpha) and is invisible over artwork this bright.

Note: this project has no Tailwind; the named utilities were translated to their computed values in
the existing CSS Module token system.

**Verification**
Headline resolves to exactly 2 lines — one per span — at 1440, 1280, 1024 and 768; 4 lines at 375,
which is unavoidable at that width. Zero horizontal overflow at every breakpoint. Line-height ratio
confirmed 1.08. `tsc --noEmit` clean.

**Contrast side-effect, consistent with the accepted trade-off above**
Widening the headline pushes its right edge from 572px to 886px, further into the fibre bundle.
Headline worst case measured 1.54 → **6.24:1** desktop (it gained, because the wider two-line
setting sits higher in the frame over darker art) but **1.14:1** on mobile. Lede **1.1:1**, note
**1.13:1**. These remain inside the "leave as is" decision recorded above — no overlay was added.

