## 2026-08-07 — Hero scrim reinstated, headline gradient added

**SUPERSEDES the "leave as is / DO NOT reintroduce a scrim" decision recorded above.** The user
explicitly requested a protective overlay this time. That earlier instruction is void; the current
state is the intended one.

**What changed** (`Hero.tsx`, `Hero.module.css`)

- **Protective scrim** (`.scrim`): absolutely positioned, `z-index: 0` — above the plate (-1) and
  below the copy, which was given `z-index: 1`. Horizontal gradient over `rgb(10 15 26)` running
  0.92 at the left to fully transparent by 82%, so the fibre bundle on the right keeps the
  brightness restored earlier. Below 900px it flips to a vertical gradient, since the copy spans
  the full width there and a left-weighted wash would do nothing.
- **Headline gradient**: `linear-gradient(to bottom right, #fff 0%, #fff 68%, #ffdcc4 86%,
  var(--ember-core) 100%)` with `background-clip: text` and a transparent fill. Every stop is fully
  opaque — a translucent stop would let the artwork read through the glyphs. The white run is held
  to 68% so only the tail of "ADDRESSES" warms, which keeps "RURAL" readable as the accent rather
  than competing with a second orange.
- **`.accent` ("RURAL")** sets `-webkit-text-fill-color` explicitly. Without it the child inherits
  the parent's transparent fill and gets painted by the headline gradient like everything else.
- **Headline shadow uses `filter: drop-shadow()`, not `text-shadow`.** With `background-clip: text`
  the glyph fill is transparent, so a text-shadow paints *behind* the letterforms and is then
  visible straight through them — it reads as a grey smear. `filter` operates on the composited
  result and shadows the clipped gradient correctly. This is the conflict the user anticipated; the
  suggested inline `textShadow` would have hit exactly that bug.
- **Lede** moved to stark white with a three-layer shadow; **actionNote** likewise. Both sized like
  `drop-shadow-lg` but with real alpha — Tailwind's default (0 4px 3px at 7%) is a card shadow and
  does nothing behind thin type over artwork.

**Corrections to the request's premises**
There was no gradient text in the hero to preserve — the headline was solid white with a flat
orange accent; the gradient is new here. The fibre animations were removed in the previous session
at the user's own instruction, so there were none to keep either.

Also note this project has no Tailwind; the named utilities were translated to computed values in
the existing CSS Module token system.

**Verification** (headless chromium, background photographed with the copy hidden)
Headline **10.75:1** desktop / **10.16:1** mobile; lede **6.0–7.02:1** / **8.75:1**; note
**10.11:1**. All clear WCAG AA. Confirmed `background-clip: text` resolved, fill transparent,
accent still `rgb(255,86,1)`, z-order plate -1 / scrim 0 / inner 1. Headline still breaks to
exactly two lines at 1440/1280/1024/768. `tsc --noEmit` clean.

