## 2026-08-07 — Hero: tightened top gap, vivid orange headline, stronger scrim

**What changed** (`Hero.module.css` only)

- **Top gap 134px → 36px.** The section was `align-items: center`, so the copy sat mid-way down an
  ~800px hero and padding alone could not close the gap — centring re-split whatever space was
  freed. Switched to `align-items: flex-start` and set top padding to
  `clamp(6rem, 5vw + 3.5rem, 7rem)`, which clears the fixed header and nothing more. Mobile gap is
  24px.
- **Headline gradient** is now `to right, #fdba74 (orange-300) → #ea580c (orange-600) → #fdba74`,
  fully opaque at every stop, on a `220%` track.
- **Sweep animation added** — `headlineSweep`, 4s linear infinite, animating `background-position`.
  There was no such animation before this change; nothing was "sped up". Disabled under
  `prefers-reduced-motion`.
- **Shadow** raised to `drop-shadow(0 4px 30px rgb(0 0 0 / 1)) drop-shadow(0 0 60px rgb(0 0 0 /
  0.8))` — the requested values, expressed as `filter` rather than `text-shadow` for the
  `background-clip: text` reason documented in the previous entry.
- **"RURAL" flipped from orange to white.** With the whole headline running orange-300 →
  orange-600, an orange accent was invisible inside it. The emphasis is inverted so the word still
  does its job.
- **Scrim** deepened to `#020617`, holding 0.9 to 40% and 0.78 to 62% before clearing by 100%;
  mobile variant raised to 0.84–0.9.

**Why the scrim went further than the literal spec**
`orange-600` is a dark colour (relative luminance 0.245), so it needs a genuinely dark backdrop.
Measured at actual glyph positions, the first attempt left the end of "ADDRESSES" at **1.31:1**
where a lit fibre showed through. The literal `from-100 via-80/50% to-transparent` ramp improved
that to 2.36:1 — still under the 3:1 floor for large text. Holding 0.78 out to 62% (just past where
the headline ends) reached **3.6:1**, and lifting the mobile variant took mobile from 2.93 to
**4.38:1**.

Note the block-box trap: an initial probe measured the h1's *layout box*, which extends past the
text into bright artwork, and reported a misleading 1.01:1. `glyph-contrast.mjs` uses
`Range.getClientRects()` so it samples only where glyphs actually sit.

**Verification**
Headline worst stop (orange-600) **3.6:1** desktop / **4.38:1** mobile; orange-300 **7.6 / 9.24**;
"RURAL" **12.82 / 15.57**; lede **19.69 / 13.7**. All clear WCAG AA for their text sizes. Sweep
confirmed running at 4s and confirmed off under reduced motion. Headline still breaks to exactly
two lines at 1440/1280/1024/768. `tsc --noEmit` clean.

**Standing caveat**
`orange-600` at 3.6:1 passes AA for large text only. It must not be used at body size, and if the
scrim is ever lightened again this stop is the first thing that breaks. Swapping the mid stop to
orange-500 or orange-400 would buy headroom if that becomes a problem.

