## 2026-08-07 — Plan card text contrast

**What changed**

An audit of every text run in `#plans` (71 runs, measured against real rendered pixels) found 8
failing WCAG AA. Two token colours were responsible:

- **`--ink-faint` (#b3b3b3) = 2.1:1 on white.** Used for "UP TO", the struck-through list prices
  ($49.99 / $69.99), the struck "$15" Wi-Fi price and the "Every plan includes" label — all at
  12–20px, where the floor is 4.5:1. Switched those to `--ink-mute` (5.28:1). Note the struck
  prices are still information a buyer needs, so making them near-invisible was the wrong call
  regardless of their decorative strikethrough.
- **`--ember-text` (#e85c0d) = 3.52:1 on white.** Fine at display size but short at 14px, where it
  was used for "$7.50/mo" and the section eyebrows. Moved those to `--ember-press` (#cc4800), the
  darkest ramp step that still reads unambiguously orange. Changed on the global `.eyebrow` too;
  sections on dark backgrounds already override it locally.

A second pass then caught 6 more on the **featured Pro card**, which the first audit had skipped
because its background is a gradient rather than a flat colour:

- **`--wash-featured` started at #ff7a2e**, only ~2.6:1 against the white text the card carries.
  Deepened to `#c94600 → #932f00` (4.8:1 and 7.9:1).
- The card's translucent whites (0.6–0.86 alpha) were raised to 0.86–0.94.

**Files touched**
`src/components/sections/PlanGrid.module.css`, `src/app/globals.css`

**Verification**
All 71 text runs in the plan cards pass. A whole-page sweep (230 runs) reports no regressions from
the two global token changes — its remaining flags are artefacts of the method (gradient-clipped
hero headline reports `rgba(0,0,0,0)`, `.sr-only` labels, and the badge's range rect).

**Separate finding, reported not fixed — out of the requested scope**
White text on the primary CTA gradient (`--wash-cta`) measures **2.64:1** against its lightest stop
`rgb(255,120,31)`. Affects three buttons: the hero address submit, the Advanced Wi-Fi CTA and the
final CTA submit. This is a genuine AA failure on the main conversion control, but it predates this
task and sits outside the plan cards, and darkening it changes every primary button on the site —
so it was raised with the user rather than changed unilaterally.

