## 2026-08-07 — Vivid hero motion

`HeroMotion` rebuilt. Previous version (Ken Burns + two blooms + a beam) was too subtle to read
against a dark photograph.

**Now running**
- **Real 3D tilt.** `perspective: 1500px` on `.hero`, `transform-style: preserve-3d` on the plate,
  and GSAP `quickTo` driving `rotationX/rotationY/scale` from pointer position. Confirmed
  resolving to `matrix3d(...)` and changing across the viewport. The plate is bled `-4%` so the
  rotation never exposes an edge.
- **Signal rings** — four SVG circles expanding out of the lit window on a 6.4s loop with staggered
  delays, stroked on an ember→cyan gradient, opacity driven per-frame in `onUpdate` so each reads
  as a pulse (fast in, slow out). Ties the motion to the fixed-wireless story rather than being
  abstract decoration.
- **14 drifting motes** on independent position and opacity loops.
- **Two Framer Motion aurora blooms** (19s / 26s) at roughly 1.5× the previous intensity, plus a
  sweeping beam.
- **`HeroReveal`** (new) — Framer entrance for the copy. The `lcp` prop marks the headline, which
  animates **transform only and never fades**: an element at `opacity: 0` is not counted as
  painted, so a fade would push LCP out by the transition length. Markup is identical under
  reduced motion (only duration changes), so it cannot desync SSR from hydration.

**Bug found and fixed**
Motes were static. `gsap.set(mote, { y: '60%' })` followed by `gsap.to(mote, { y: '-=40' })` mixes
percentage and pixel units and GSAP cannot resolve the relative step. Placement moved to
`left`/`top`, motion to transforms from zero. All 14 confirmed moving.

**Verification**
All layers confirmed animating (plate, image, aurora, beam, rings, 14/14 motes); pointer tilt
confirmed responding. Reduced motion renders the layer not at all. Headline opacity stays 1
throughout entry. **LCP 692ms, CLS 0.0019.** Hero contrast measured with the cursor parked over the
copy: headline **16.59:1**, lede **19.41:1** — well up on the previous 5.57/4.99, because the
plate bleed moved darker artwork under the text. Responsive clean. `tsc --noEmit` clean.

Note: `Hero_plate` and `HeroMotion_root` both exceed the viewport by design (negative inset) and
are clipped by `.hero { overflow: hidden }`. Added to the responsive script's clipped-ancestor
exclusion alongside the marquee.

---

## 2026-08-07 — Hero signal rings removed

The concentric rings read as a target/ripple sitting oddly over a photograph of a room. Refined
them first into broadcast arcs anchored off the top-right corner (semantically right for fixed
wireless), but that did not work either, for a structural reason worth recording:

**Screen-blended strokes need a dark backdrop.** This hero is a dark room on the left and a bright
lit window on the right. Over the window the arcs were invisible at any tasteful weight; anywhere
dark enough to show them is the left third, which the headline occupies. Pushed bright enough to
register, they returned to looking like a target.

Removed rather than left in as dead weight. The hero's motion is now carried by the 3D pointer
tilt, the Ken Burns push, two aurora blooms, the sweeping beam and 14 drifting motes — all
verified still running, with reduced motion still rendering the layer not at all.

`hero-vivid`/`hero-arcs` screenshots in the scratchpad show the before/after if this is revisited.
If some signal motif is wanted later, it needs to live somewhere with a dark background — the
TrustBand or the Final CTA would both take one.

---

## 2026-08-07 — Hero motion made actually visible (`HeroGrid`)

**Why three previous attempts failed.** Every ambient effect so far — aurora blooms, motes, beam,
signal rings, arcs — was rendered by `HeroMotion` at `z-index: -1`, i.e. *beneath* the contrast
scrim. That scrim runs at **0.9–1.0 alpha across the left of the hero**, which is precisely the
dark region where a screen-blended effect could otherwise be seen. Nothing drawn under it there is
visible at any brightness. The hero kept reading as static because, in the areas that mattered, it
was.

**`components/hero/HeroGrid.tsx` (new)** renders **above** the scrim (`z-index: 0`, after it in the
DOM), below the copy:
- **Receding 3D perspective grid** along the floor of the frame — `perspective: 320px` with the
  plane at `rotateX(72deg)`, its `background-position` scrolled on a 5.5s GSAP loop so the lines
  travel toward the horizon. Masked out before it reaches the address form.
- **Three data streams** — SVG curves with `strokeDasharray` sized to 18% of path length and
  `strokeDashoffset` animated, so light runs along each. Masked to the left ~52% where the artwork
  is near-black; over the lit window on the right they would be invisible anyway.
- **Travelling pulses** riding the same curves via `getPointAtLength`, opacity following a sine so
  each fades in and out across its run.

`HeroMotion` stays below the scrim for what genuinely belongs there (the plate's 3D pointer tilt,
Ken Burns, aurora, beam, motes).

**Verification**
Grid scroll, stream flow and pulse travel all confirmed changing between samples; nothing renders
under reduced motion; zero page errors. Contrast unmoved — headline **16.4:1**, lede **12.5:1**
worst case with the cursor over the copy. **LCP 1392ms, CLS 0.0014.** Responsive clean.

`HeroGrid_floor` / `HeroGrid_grid` exceed the viewport by design (negative inset plus rotation) and
are clipped by the layer's `overflow: hidden`; added to the responsive script's exclusion list
alongside `Hero_plate`, `HeroMotion_root` and the marquee.

**Rule:** anything meant to be seen in the hero's left two thirds must render above the scrim.

---

## 2026-08-07 — Hero overlays removed for good

Removed `HeroGrid` (perspective grid + data streams + pulses) and the drifting motes from
`HeroMotion`. Thin diagonal strokes over a photograph of a living room read as scratches or render
artifacts, and the motes read as dust specks or dead pixels — not as design.

**The pattern across four failed attempts** — signal rings, broadcast arcs, data streams, motes:
this hero's background is a literal photograph of a domestic interior. Abstract tech-graphic
overlays fight it rather than enhance it, and no amount of tuning position, weight or opacity
changes that. Anything visible enough to register looks like damage to the photo; anything subtle
enough not to, is invisible.

**What remains, and why it works:** motion applied to the photograph itself rather than drawn on
top of it — GSAP 3D pointer tilt against the hero's perspective, a slow Ken Burns push, two aurora
blooms that shift the colour of the scene, a soft light beam, and the Framer copy entrance. That
reads as cinematography, which a photographic hero can carry.

**Do not add particle, line, grid, ring or mesh overlays to this hero.** Four attempts, all
rejected. If a tech motif is wanted, it belongs on a section with a flat dark background —
TrustBand or the Final CTA.

**Verification:** zero stray overlay elements in the DOM; Ken Burns, aurora, beam and pointer tilt
all confirmed still animating; zero console errors; `tsc --noEmit` clean.

---

## 2026-08-07 — `HeroBackground.tsx` — hero backdrop consolidated into four layers

Replaces `HeroMotion` (deleted) and the plate/scrim markup that lived in `Hero.tsx`. One typed
client component now owns the entire backdrop; `Hero.tsx` passes the image and a `depth` prop.

**Layers, back to front**
0. **Mesh gradient** — three offset radials (deep indigo, dark ember, muted magenta) over `#070a14`,
   `background-position` animated across a 34s Framer loop. Kept dark: it sits under the photograph
   and only has to stop the fill reading as flat.
1. **Photograph** — parallax wrapper driven by `useMotionValue` → `useTransform` → `useSpring`
   (stiffness 60 / damping 20 / mass 0.8), mapped to a **negative** range so the plate drifts
   against the cursor. Inset `-24px` so the offset never exposes an edge. Inside it, the image runs
   `scale: [1, 1.05, 1]` over 40s linear infinite.
2. **Laptop glow** — bloom mapped to the screen in the lower-right of the frame, `blur(100px)`,
   `mix-blend-mode: screen`, opacity pulsing `[0.3, 0.6, 0.4, 0.7, 0.3]` over 4.2s on uneven
   `times` so it reads as flicker rather than a sine.
3. **Film grain** — inline SVG `feTurbulence` at `opacity: 0.05`. No network request, no asset.
4. **Contrast scrim** — *not in the brief, and deliberately kept.* Without it the headline measures
   1.2:1 against the artwork. Holds 0.9 to 40% and 0.78 to 62% because the headline runs to ~62% of
   the viewport; fading earlier put its darkest gradient stop at 1.31:1 on a lit area. Goes
   vertical below 900px where the copy spans full width.

Content sits at `z-index: 10`.

**Reduced motion** collapses transition durations and zeroes the parallax travel — it never changes
what renders. `useReducedMotion` returns null during SSR, so branching markup on it strands
elements in their initial style (this bit the plan cards earlier).

**LCP** is protected: the image keeps `priority`, and the headline animates transform only,
never opacity.

Note: this project has no Tailwind. The requested utilities (`opacity-[0.05]`, `blur-[100px]`,
`bg-blue-400/20`, `mix-blend-screen`, `relative z-10`) were translated to their computed values in
the existing CSS Module token system.

**Verification**
All four layers confirmed animating; under reduced motion mesh, Ken Burns, parallax and glow all
confirmed off while grain and scrim still render. Contrast held — headline **17.9:1**, lede
**19.34:1** worst case with the cursor over the copy. **LCP 1840ms, CLS 0.0014.** Responsive clean.
Zero console errors. `tsc --noEmit` clean.

**Addendum — glowing dots (left edge)**
22 dots added to `HeroBackground` at `z-index: 5`, above the scrim. Positions are hand-authored and
weighted to `x < 26%`, with a radial mask (`70% 90% at 0% 50%`) so density falls away before the
photograph starts. Blue and warm variants, three-stop box-shadow halos, each on its own 7–13s
opacity and drift loop with staggered delays.

This is the same idea as the motes that were removed earlier, and it works here for one reason:
placement. Over the photograph they read as dust on the lens; against the flat near-black left
corner they read as ambient light. Confirmed all 22 sit inside the left third.

Contrast unmoved: headline **17.76:1**, lede **19.34:1**. Frozen under reduced motion (0 of 22
animating). Responsive clean.

**Addendum — dots scattered full-frame, network clusters added**
- Dots extended to **34**, spread L22 / C6 / R6 across the three thirds. The layer mask changed
  from a left-only radial to a horizontal ramp holding full strength to 34% and easing to 0.4 at
  the right edge — at full brightness over the photograph they read as dust on the lens, so the
  right half is deliberately dimmer.
- **Two sparse network clusters** (`.nets`), five nodes and five edges each: one in the dark left
  corner, one centre-frame. Hairline edges at `0.09` stroke with `vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke`
  so they stay 1px at any viewport. Nodes pulse on staggered 5–9s loops. Layer opacity 0.5 and
  masked out entirely past 88%, so nothing lands on the lit part of the photograph.

Both sit at `z-index: 5`, above the scrim — the only place effects are visible in the dark left
region.

Contrast unmoved: headline **17.9:1**, lede **19.34:1**. Both layers frozen under reduced motion.
Responsive clean.

